      "model": "zai/glm-5.3",
      "question": "We filtered the oil on Transformer 1 at site MAIN in early January, and I need a clean pre-filtration baseline to compare against. Pull the hydrogen, methane, ethylene, acetylene and co readings for every oil sample taken in the two months before 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000. Give me the min, max and mean of each gas over that pre-January period, and the average daily rise in ppm/day for hydrogen and acetylene. Then tell me by what percentage each gas is lower in the latest sample than in that baseline, so I can judge how much the filtration is masking the fault gases.",
      "answer": "I can't build the baseline you asked for \u2014 **there is no pre-filtration data for Transformer 1**.\n\nWhat the telemetry shows:\n\n- **Total records for the asset:** only 5, spanning **2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19** (daily samples, one per day).\n- **Query for the two months before 2024-01-15 (2023-11-15 \u2192 2024-01-15):** returned **0 observations** for hydrogen, methane, ethylene, acetylene, and CO. The earliest sample of any kind is dated 2024-01-15, i.e. the day of (or right after) the filtration event.\n- Consequently, pre-January min/max/mean and hydrogen/acetylene daily rise rates are **not computable** \u2014 there is no \"before\" window in the data.\n\nWhat is available (the **latest** sample, 2024-01-19):\n- Hydrogen: 2,850 ppm\n- Methane: 7,400 ppm\n- Ethylene: 16,650 ppm\n- Acetylene: 7 ppm\n- CO: 32 ppm\n\nNote these are extremely elevated levels (ethylene 16,650 ppm with acetylene present suggests severe thermal faulting in oil), so even the post-filtration values indicate an active fault, not a \"clean\" condition.\n\nIf you have lab reports or DGA results from before mid-January stored elsewhere (e.g., in an external file), I can analyze those if you provide them \u2014 or I can run a trend analysis across the 5 available post-filtration samples (Jan 15\u201319) to see whether gas levels are rising despite filtration. Would that help?",
